    Bug 355205 - Modernize tag removal to process only applied tags. r=mkmelin


    Previously, `_removeAllMessageTags` blindly sent the entire global dictionary
    of known tags to the backend to be removed from the selected messages. For
    IMAP accounts, this generated massive `-FLAGS` payloads. Worse, if a user
    had even one malformed tag anywhere in their profile (e.g., auto-generated
    from an RSS feed with an `&`), the IMAP server would reject the entire batch
    command, causing a permanent local/server state desync where tags could no
    longer be removed.

    This commit refactors the frontend tag toggling and removal logic:

    * Smart Intersection: `_removeAllMessageTags` now inspects the `keywords`
      property of the selected messages. It only asks the backend to remove tags
      that are actually applied to the message *and* recognized by
      `MailServices.tags` (which safely preserves internal system flags like
      `$Junk`).

    * Robust Folder Grouping: `_toggleMessageTag` and `_removeAllMessageTags` now
      use ES6 `Map`s to group selected messages by folder. This replaces the
      fragile consecutive-folder loop and flawlessly handles cross-folder
      selections in Unified/Virtual folders.

    * Dead Code Removal: Strips out the legacy Thunderbird 1.x `msgHdr.label`
      integer fallback logic, as the underlying XPCOM attribute was completely
      removed in Bug 1802815.

    Differential Revision: https://phabricator.services.mozilla.com/D302293

